<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Mosquito Reserve</h1><p class="st-meta">1860 CE – 1894 CE · capital Bluefields</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Autonomous Miskito territory on Nicaragua's Caribbean coast, created by a British-Nicaraguan treaty in 1860 and annexed by Nicaragua in 1894.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> had long protected the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mosquito-kingdom">Miskito Kingdom</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, but under American pressure it agreed to withdraw. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-managua">Treaty of Managua</a> of 1860 recognised Nicaraguan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> while creating a self-governing reserve for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskito">Miskito people</a>, with its seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bluefields">Bluefields</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The reserve was run by a hereditary chief, as the former king was now styled, and a council and assembly whose officials were often <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">Creoles</a> of African descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bluefields">Bluefields</a>. English was the language of government, and Moravian missionaries, active since 1849, spread Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and schooling. An Austrian imperial arbitration in 1881 upheld the reserve's autonomy. American banana, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> firms grew powerful in Bluefields in the 1880s.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1894 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-santos-zelaya">José Santos Zelaya</a> sent troops under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rigoberto-cabezas">Rigoberto Cabezas</a> to occupy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bluefields">Bluefields</a>, and the territory was reincorporated as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zelaya-department">Zelaya</a>. The last chief, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-henry-clarence">Robert Henry Clarence</a>, went into exile in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> recognised Nicaraguan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in 1905, and coastal autonomy remains a live issue.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1860 CE – 1894 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">other</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.72%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1860 CE – 1894 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bluefields</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Robert Henry Clarence</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Moravian Church</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Miskito and English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Reserva Mosquitia</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1877&amp;focus=mosquito-reserve" aria-label="Mosquito Reserve on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2378 1730 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mosquito Reserve lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2698 1986L2698 1986M2698 1986L2668 1949"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2698 1986h0M2668 1949h0"/><p